<div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 24 Leadership Directory</h1>
    <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 24. Understanding the roles and responsibilities of each contact point is crucial for effective advocacy for your child's special education needs.</p>

    <div class="contact-grid">

        <div class="contact-box">
            <h3>CSE 4 (Serving District 24)</h3>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>28-11 Queens Plaza North, LIC, NY 11101</div>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-391-8405">718-391-8405</a></div>
            <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #777; margin-top: 10px;">
                <strong>CSE Responsibilities:</strong> This CSE handles initial referrals, evaluations, IEP development, and placement recommendations for students with disabilities in District 24. They are responsible for ensuring compliance with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Part 200 Regulations.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
        <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Keep meticulous records of all communication attempts, including dates, times, methods of contact (phone, email), and the names of individuals contacted. This documentation is invaluable if you need to file a formal complaint.
    </div>


    <section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
        </h2>

        <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window outlined in NYCRR 200.5(a)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This is especially critical when requesting an Initial Referral or an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE).
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary. Confirm the date, time, and key points discussed.</li>
                    <li>Use a clear subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"Request for Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) - [Student Name]"</em></li>
                    <li>Keep a detailed "Communication Log" of dates, names, methods of contact, and a brief summary of the conversation. This is crucial evidence if disputes arise.</li>
                    <li>When emailing, always BCC yourself to have a record of your sent communication.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder. Remember to document each step meticulously.
                </p>
                <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Clearly state your request and the urgency.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal and any relevant teacher). Reiterate your request and reference your previous communication.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. State that you have not received a response despite multiple attempts and that you are concerned about the potential impact on your child's education.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or if the district fails to adhere to mandated timelines (e.g., failing to hold an IEP meeting within a reasonable timeframe after a request). The NYSED complaint form can be found on their website. Be sure to attach all supporting documentation.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="iep-tips" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> IEP Advocacy Tips for District 24
        </h2>

        <div class="tip-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(350px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

            <div class="tip-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Preparation is Key</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    Before attending an IEP meeting, thoroughly review your child's evaluation reports, progress reports, and any other relevant documentation. Prepare a list of questions and concerns you want to address. Bring a written statement outlining your goals for your child's education.
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Draft your Parent Statement prior to the IEP meeting. This is your opportunity to clearly articulate your perspective and concerns.</li>
                    <li>Review the Present Levels of Performance (PLOP) section of the IEP carefully. Ensure it accurately reflects your child's strengths and weaknesses.</li>
                    <li>Come prepared with specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART) goals you would like to see included in the IEP.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="tip-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Understand Your Rights</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    Familiarize yourself with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Part 200 Regulations. You have the right to participate fully in the IEP process, to request evaluations, to disagree with the IEP, and to pursue dispute resolution options.
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Know your rights regarding Independent Educational Evaluations (IEEs). You have the right to request an IEE at public expense if you disagree with the district's evaluation. (NYCRR 200.5(g))</li>
                    <li>Understand the "stay put" provision. If you disagree with a proposed change to your child's IEP, your child has the right to remain in their current placement while the dispute is being resolved.</li>
                    <li>Be aware of your options for dispute resolution, including mediation, impartial hearings, and state complaints.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="tip-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Document Everything</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    Keep meticulous records of all communication with the school district, including emails, letters, and phone calls. Document all IEP meetings and any agreements made. This documentation will be invaluable if you need to pursue dispute resolution.
                </p>
                 <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>After each IEP meeting, send a follow-up email to the CSE team summarizing the key discussion points and any agreements reached.</li>
                    <li>Request copies of all evaluation reports, IEPs, and other relevant documents.</li>
                    <li>Maintain a chronological file of all documents related to your child's special education.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="tip-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Don't Be Afraid to Advocate</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    You are your child's best advocate. Don't be afraid to ask questions, express your concerns, and challenge the school district if you believe your child's needs are not being met. Consider bringing a parent advocate or attorney to IEP meetings for support.
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>If you feel overwhelmed or intimidated, seek support from a parent advocacy organization or a special education attorney.</li>
                    <li>Remember that the IEP is a collaborative process. Work with the school district to develop an IEP that meets your child's individual needs.</li>
                    <li>If you disagree with the IEP, don't sign it. Instead, write a letter to the CSE outlining your concerns.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is the difference between the CSE and CPSE?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">The Committee on Special Education (CSE) is responsible for students aged 5-21 who are suspected of having a disability. The Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) is responsible for children aged 3-5. District 24 primarily interacts with CSE 4 as noted above, but CPSE involvement is essential for younger children residing within the district.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What if I disagree with the school's evaluation of my child?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">You have the right to obtain an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the school's evaluation. You must request the IEE in writing. The district must either pay for the IEE or initiate an impartial hearing to demonstrate that their evaluation was appropriate. (NYCRR 200.5(g))</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">How often is the IEP reviewed?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">The IEP must be reviewed at least annually. However, you have the right to request an IEP meeting at any time if you believe your child's needs are not being met. (IDEA, 34 CFR §300.324)</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is an Impartial Hearing?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">An impartial hearing is a formal legal proceeding where you can present evidence and arguments to challenge the school district's decisions regarding your child's special education. You have the right to be represented by an attorney at the hearing. An Impartial Hearing Officer (IHO) will render a decision. This is governed by NYCRR 200.5(k).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">My child is not receiving the services outlined in their IEP. What should I do?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">First, contact the CSE and the school principal in writing to document the lack of services. If the issue is not resolved, you can file a State Complaint with NYSED. You may also consider pursuing mediation or an impartial hearing. Document everything, including dates, times, and names of people you spoke with.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

</div>
</main>
